3:One reference for coarse-grained multi-adapter routing in Granite Switch, as the code stands today: how the two attention heads compute an adapter index per token, what the HF and vLLM backends do differently, how multi-turn conversations reuse KV, and which limits are real. Every number below was read from the source or produced by running it.
8:2. [The engine: two attention heads and a token rewrite](#2-the-engine-two-attention-heads-and-a-token-rewrite)
18:> **If you read only this box.** MultiSwitch replaced the earlier SingleSwitch (now removed) and its single-transition routing with a *coded memory*: two tiny attention heads turn "how many control tokens have I seen?" into an address, and read back the adapter written at that address. Routing is piecewise-constant, latest-wins, and a pure function of the token ids. The control token is then rewritten to a substitute id so the decoder never embeds it. It is the only engine (Kerdock/DG codes); two backends implement it; the only substantive behavioural difference between them is arithmetic precision, which caps a vLLM request at **188 retained control tokens**.
46:| Mechanism | +/-gain cumsum over one attention head | counting head + Kerdock/DG coded memory head |
48:| KV-cache slots consumed | 1 | 2 (counting + memory) |
60:| `ms_counting_head_dim` | `32` | counting head dim; floored at 32 for FlashAttention | `config.py:78,132` |
70:| `codebook` | `[2048, 64]` fp32 unit-norm codewords, ~512 KB | `from_pretrained` materializes modules on the meta device and fills only tensors present in the checkpoint. A non-persistent buffer comes back **all zeros**, which makes every memory logit 0, the retrieval softmax uniform, and each position average the expert ids it can see instead of selecting the latest (`hf/switch/multi.py:209-220`). |
78:num_decoder_layers = config.num_hidden_layers - layer_offset
81:## 2. The engine: two attention heads and a token rewrite
83:There is no router network and no learned parameter. The whole engine is arithmetic dressed as two single-head attention calls, so it inherits the backend's batching, masking and KV cache for free.
92:  │  every control token holds   K unmasked, V = 0   │        │
103:  │            ──►  softmax concentrates on address n│        │
139:Position 0 is the anchor. It is derived from `cache_position` / `positions`, not from a special `<|init|>` token, and it is not hidden from the decoder:
146:Then the three tensors. Keys default to a mask value and are un-masked only at the anchor and at control tokens; values are 1 only at the anchor; queries are one-hot on dim 0, so `Q.K` reduces to `K[0]`:
150:key_states_count   = full((B,1,S,32), -1e9)               # masked by default
151:key_states_count[:,0,:,0] = where(anchor_or_control, 0, -1e9)  # un-mask participants
166:> IEEE-754 defines `0 * +/-inf = NaN`, and the one-hot query has zeros at exactly the dimensions where the mask sits. `-1e9` gives `0 * -1e9 = 0` and still `exp(-1e9) ~= 0` in the softmax, so it masks without poisoning the product (`hf/switch/multi.py:72-76`, `vllm/switch/multi.py:108-112`).
177:key_states_memory[:,0,:,:64]   = all_code_vectors * 28.0 * write_mask   # control tokens only
182:Kerdock/DG codewords have provably low mutual coherence, so a query of `code(n)` against keys of `code(m) * 28` produces a softmax that concentrates on `m == n`. The attended value is the `expert_id` most recently written at address `n`; `round` and `clamp[0, num_adapters]` make it an integer index (`hf/switch/multi.py:539-541`).
202:> MultiSwitch does not use the group-based KV-hiding path. `grep -rn hiding src/` returns two comments and no mechanism; the control token is neutralised by having its id replaced before embedding, which is why `modified_ids` above shows `11` and `12` where the control tokens were.
245:The two files are deliberate twins -- same stages, same order, same masking constant, and the codes and token-exchange helpers are imported from the HF side as the single source of truth (`vllm/switch/multi.py:99-106`). What differs is the attention they call, and therefore the dtype they are allowed to compute in.
249:| Attention | `ALL_ATTENTION_FUNCTIONS["sdpa"]`, hand-built Q/K/V | two real `vllm.Attention` modules (`switch.multi.0`, `switch.multi.1`) |
251:| Backend override | SDPA forced for **both** heads, not the model's configured backend | whatever attention backend vLLM selected |
253:| Causal mask | built internally in fp32 over `kv_len`, ignoring the model's mask | vLLM's own attention metadata |
254:| KV cache | `DynamicCache` slots `layer_idx` / `layer_idx + 1` | paged KV cache, two cache groups |
262:1. **The model's bf16 attention mask.** Feeding a bf16 mask to fp32 Q/K/V makes SDPA run the whole attention in bf16; the recovered `n` comes out off by one, so routing lags a position -- `[T,A,T,B,T]` gives `[0,0,0,1,1]` instead of `[0,1,1,2,2]` (`:405-416`).
263:2. **The model's configured backend.** An earlier version dispatched the memory head through `config._attn_implementation`. flash_attention_2 requires fp16/bf16, silently downcast, destroyed the codebook separation, and routed every token to base -- the isolated switch tests passed while the full model returned all-zero indices (`:392-402`).
264:3. **Enclosing bf16 autocast.** The memory keys are `code(n) * 28` and the mask is large and negative; in bf16 these overflow the softmax to NaN intermittently, per kernel, which rounds and clamps to 0 (`:442-449`).
266:The HF mask is also built over the *key* length, not the query length. That is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: at a decode step with `q_len=1` and `kv_len=6`, a `q_len x q_len` mask made SDPA raise `"(*bias): last dimension must be contiguous"` -- a shape complaint, not a stride problem. Every forward-only test passed because prefill has `q_len == kv_len` (`:417-436`).
270:Its heads are paged-KV attention modules, so their Q/K/V dtype is fixed by the engine's KV-cache dtype. Serving a bf16 checkpoint quantizes the `1/(1+n)` signal in the cache, and 188 is the arithmetic consequence rather than an oversight. That is section 8's first limit.
274:The switch writes none of vLLM's per-request machinery. It builds constant Q/K/V and calls `vllm.Attention`, which means per-request isolation is inherited, not implemented.
289:                 (attention confined per request by vLLM, not by us)
303:### Decode carries the adapter through the cache
305:At a decode step the control token is long gone from `input_ids`. Routing still works because both heads are real paged-KV attention: the single new query attends over the cached anchor and control-token keys, recovers the same `n`, and reads back the same expert id. The cache is the mechanism, not an obstacle.
321:| `is_counting_anchor`, `is_control_token`, `expert_ids`, all six Q/K/V tensors, `write_addresses`, `adapter_indices` | the two heads' keys and values, in the paged KV cache, under vLLM's own block tables |
327:> vLLM reuses a cached block when **the token prefix leading to it is identical**. Nothing else is consulted -- not the adapter, not the turn, not the request. And because adapter routing is computed from those same tokens, identical tokens always mean identical routing, so a reused block is always exactly what recomputing would have produced.
329:Adapters are weights inside the checkpoint selected by control tokens in the stream, so they are invisible to the cache key: `hash_block_tokens` mixes the parent hash, the block's token ids, and `extra_keys` (vLLM LoRA name, multimodal ids, `cache_salt`, prompt-embeds digest) -- none of which Granite Switch sets.
343:> "Switching adapter invalidates the cache" is backwards. Appending the *new* turn's control token is harmless -- routing is causal, so nothing before it moves. **Losing the old one is the whole story.**
